#{[17592186148124 :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=4{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3})=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2x+7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+7)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2x+7$ gives an exact equation."] [17592186178563 :answer-field/type #edb/ref 17592186045492] [17592186178563 :answer-field/id #uuid "6e1e465c-e525-5665-8b77-e73f57338bdf"]}
